findings.body:
In the recurring-events branch the code does:
```
bookingToDelete.user.credentials.filter(...).forEach(async (credential) => {
  const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
  for (const updBooking of updatedBookings) {
    ...
    const deletedEvent = await calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId);
    apiDeletes.push(deletedEvent);
  }
});
```
`forEach` does not await async callbacks, so `apiDeletes` is still (or mostly) empty when execution reaches `await Promise.all(prismaPromises.concat(apiDeletes))` later in the function. The recurring calendar events are not actually deleted; only the local DB rows are cancelled. Use `await Promise.all(...map(async ...))` or a sequential `for...of` loop.

The legacy pre-refactor branch was rewritten from
```
bookingToDelete.user.credentials.filter(...).forEach((credential) => {
  const calendar = getCalendar(credential);
  apiDeletes.push(calendar?.deleteEvent(uid, evt, externalCalendarId) as Promise<unknown>);
});
```
to a `for (const credential of calendarCredentials) { const calendar = await getCalendar(credential); apiDeletes.push(calendar?.deleteEvent(...)); }` loop. Because of the `await`, calendars are now deleted strictly one after another instead of in parallel, multiplying wall-clock time when users have multiple connected calendars.
